# Line endings.
#
# Git for Windows ships core.autocrlf=true in its system config, which converts
# LF to CRLF on checkout. Every file here is already stored as LF, so that
# conversion produced "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warnings on almost every
# `git add` and left the working tree disagreeing with the repository.
#
# This file settles it for the repo rather than per machine: checked in, so a
# clone on any OS behaves the same regardless of the local autocrlf setting.
#
# text=auto  — Git decides what is text, and normalizes it to LF on commit.
# eol=lf     — and checks it back out as LF, including on Windows.
#
# LF everywhere is the right call for this project: it is a static site of
# HTML/CSS/JS/JSON deployed to Vercel (Linux), every editor involved handles LF,
# and keeping the working tree byte-identical to the index means no conversion,
# no warnings, and no whitespace-only diffs when a file is touched on a
# different machine.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary files must never be touched. Listed explicitly rather than relying on
# Git's content heuristic, which can misread a small binary as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.eot binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
